{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba collab/1790343500000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-rereview.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790344000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba collab/1790343500000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-rereview.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 14:41:16 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 21:41:16 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\tRead-only revision; no files edited. Codex's four new P1s are correct on the tree, and I accept the three-change split. The orchestrator's question is the important one, and the answer changes the shape of the design: the boundary table was enumerating boundaries for the wrong property. Soundness rests on two functions that already exist, not on a list of loaders. Details below, then the revised items.\n     5\t\n     6\t## Verdicts on the new P1s and the three partly-resolved items\n     7\t\n     8\t**P1, semantic datom-code loader: ACCEPT.** `semantic/vm-load-program` decodes a datom batch through `load-image` (semantic.cljc:670, 529), which validates with `code/well-formed?` (code.cljc:162), a different rule set from `code/well-formed-vector?` (code.cljc:370). Its `:var` at run time resolves through the shared resolver (semantic.cljc:263). Both validators gain the reserved-operand and `:define` rules. Under the structural argument below, that is early refusal, not the proof.\n     9\t\n    10\t**P1, module-store snapshots and scheduler updates: PARTLY.** Snapshots: there is no code. A search of the source tree for `module-stores` or `store-of` finds nothing; the restore into `:module-stores` exists only in the spec (linker.md 1342). Codex says as much. It becomes a stated obligation on the M4 implementer, discharged by the store-write choke point below rather than by a new check. Scheduler updates: the merge at engine.cljc:425 takes `:store-updates` whose keys are either cursor ids the engine minted through `gen-id` as keywords (engine.cljc:79, 150) or transport-supplied entries keyed by those same ids. dao.await's keys are minted keywords too (await.cljc:178). No program symbol can enter that path, so no shadow route exists there. I add one assertion at the merge as a regression tripwire, not as a boundary. Carried environments: a restored env binding `yin/def` is inert once the resolver refuses the name before reading env, so no env check is needed for soundness.\n    11\t\n    12\t**P1, old-image refusal: ACCEPT, and I retract the sentence.** \"An old lowered image contains `:var yin/def`\" holds only for images with a definition. Further, the stack loader does not validate at all (stack.cljc:104 assigns the segment directly; only the linker's admission runs `image-defect`), the register loader validates structure only (register.cljc:106), the semantic loaders take no contract (semantic.cljc:713, 728), and the M2 fetch admits an omitted contract (linker.cljc:1190). Plan in item 3.\n    13\t\n    14\t**P1, UCF criteria: ACCEPT.** The UCF namespace holds canonicalization and safepoint helpers only; its public functions end at `unsatisfied-names` (ucf.cljc:387) and there is no lift or lower anywhere in the tree. The ledger's profile comparison (ledger.cljc:197) is a derivation check, not a continuation check. Criteria 7's continuation half and criterion 10 move to M4. A local parked-during-definition resume test replaces 10 in this gate.\n    15\t\n    16\t**Direct admission, partly resolved: ACCEPT** as above. **Effect and store ingress: PARTLY** as above. **Contract revisions: ACCEPT**, plan in item 3.\n    17\t\n    18\t## 1. The choke-point question\n    19\t\n    20\tThe orchestrator is right, and codex's rounds show why: each round found another loader because the design was trying to prove soundness at admission, and admission has many doors. The two facts Rule R needs are owned by two functions that every door already leads to.\n    21\t\n    22\t**Fact one, no shadowing.** Every name resolution in every engine goes through `engine/resolve-var`: the walker (ast_walker.cljc:379 and 628), the semantic VM (semantic.cljc:265), the stack VM (stack.cljc:386), the register VM (register.cljc:390), and the UCF conformance check (ucf.cljc:387). Round one verified this, codex confirmed it. If `resolve-var` refuses a reserved name before it consults env, then no loader, no supplied env, no store entry, no ready-queue merge, and no snapshot can make a reserved name resolve to anything, on any path, admitted or not. A lambda parameter named `yin/def` becomes unreadable rather than dangerous. The definition transition never calls the resolver for its operator. Those two changes are the whole soundness proof. The table of loaders becomes early, friendlier refusal, and a loader that is missing from it costs a late error, not a hole.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Fact two, the store never holds the key.** This is a tidiness invariant, and codex already agreed it cannot affect a syntactic definition. It has one honest shape: a single `engine/store-put` function that refuses reserved keys, used by `handle-effect` (engine.cljc:511) and by the four direct instructions that today assoc into the store themselves (walker 419 to 438, semantic opcode 11 at semantic.cljc:303, stack.cljc:482, register.cljc:481). The ingress merges (constructors, the ready-queue merge, dao.await, the future module-store lower) are state constructors, not code paths, and are closed by a lint rule: no `assoc` into `:store` outside `yin.vm.engine`. That rule is checkable by grep in the gate, so the invariant does not depend on anyone remembering a boundary.\n    25\t\n    26\tWhy there is no fourth round: the table no longer carries the proof. A new loader, engine, or restore path cannot reintroduce shadowing without either bypassing `resolve-var`, which would already break every other name, or writing the store outside `engine/store-put`, which the lint refuses.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. Revised Rule R and completeness condition\n    29\t\n    30\tThe rule by role is unchanged from revision one: a `yin/def` variable occurrence is legal only as the operator of a two-operand application with a literal-symbol key other than `yin/def`; the name is not a binder, not a store key, not a definition or macro key; quoted data is allowed.\n    31\t\n    32\tEnforcement, now in three tiers:\n    33\t\n    34\t- **Proof tier, transition time.** `resolve-var` refuses the name before env lookup. The definition transition in each engine, and the `:define` opcodes, never resolve the operator. `engine/store-put` refuses the key. These three are the completeness condition.\n    35\t- **Early-refusal tier, load time.** `vm/validate-rows` and `semantic-bytecode->ast` (occurrence-aware, whole tree), `code/well-formed?` and `code/well-formed-vector?`, `image-defect` and `register-image-defect`, and the four linker records that wrap them. Stack `load-image` starts calling `image-defect`, a pre-existing defect fixed here.\n    36\t- **Ingress tier, construction time.** Each `create-vm` refuses a supplied store, env, or registry binding the name; the expander's `make-ctx` and `expand-batch` refuse a store binding it; harvest refuses the key; the ready-queue merge asserts non-symbol keys; the M4 module-store lower goes through `engine/store-put`.\n    37\t\n    38\tCompleteness condition, stated once: **a program is sound with respect to definitions if every name it resolves passes through `resolve-var` and every store write passes through `engine/store-put`.** Both are true of the tree by inspection today for resolution, and by the lint after the four direct writes are rerouted.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 3. Old-image refusal\n    41\t\n    42\tStamps are required wherever persistent code is admitted. The runtime backstop is the resolver: an old image with a definition throws `:reserved-name` at its first `:var yin/def`, so a mis-stamped image cannot shadow, only fail.\n    43\t\n    44\t- **Linker fetch.** An omitted contract becomes `:invalid-request`; a differing one stays `:contract-mismatch`. Callers pass the record's contract explicitly.\n    45\t- **Manifests and derivations.** `:yin.module/contracts` and the ledger profile move to \"v3\", \"v3\", \"b2\", \"r2\"; a manifest naming an old revision is refused at the existing 8.1 check.\n    46\t- **Direct loaders.** The walker's datom and row loaders, the semantic datom and vector loaders, and the stack and register `load-image` each take a required `:contract` and refuse `:contract-missing` or `:contract-mismatch` before validation. A datom batch carries it on the segment entity beside the existing hash claim.\n    47\t- **Fresh source.** Yang, the linearizers, and the expander stamp their output with the current constant in `yin.vm`. A loader call without a stamp is refused, not defaulted, so fresh source is an explicit producer-stamps path, distinct from any claim about old images.\n    48\t- **Old images with no definition.** Refused by stamp. Their bytes may be interpretable, but a revision is a property of interpretation, and refusing is cheaper than proving harmlessness per image.\n    49\t- **Continuations.** Stamp comparison at lower is M4, since lower does not exist.\n    50\t\n    51\t## 4. The three-change split: ACCEPT\n    52\t\n    53\t**Change A, compatible core.** `resolve-var` reads reserved names from the registry only, skipping env and store; `engine/store-put` introduced and used by all five write sites, refusing the key; constructor, expander-context, and harvest refusals; the ready-queue assertion; the lint. `yin/def` stays in the registry so definitions still work. One caveat held on evidence: strictly, UCF 7.3.3 calls a resolution-order change a new revision. The only programs that observe change A are ones that exercised a shadow route, which had no defined meaning. I recommend recording A as an erratum to the current revisions; if codex holds to the strict reading, A folds into B and the split is two. Gate: tri-host lanes; tests per host: the shadow, store-put, computed-key, and alias fixtures all leave a later definition intact; a supplied store or env with the name is refused; a seeded macro named `yin/def` is refused at context construction; the lint passes.\n    54\t\n    55\t**Change B, atomic cutover.** Definition transition in all four engines, `:define` opcodes, `yin/def` removed from the registry, `resolve-var` refusing unconditionally before env, the load-time validators, required loader contracts, stamps to \"v3\", \"v3\", \"b2\", \"r2\" in `yin.vm`, ledger, and UCF constant, producer stamping, stack loader validation, `free-names` excluding the operator, all docs. Gate: tri-host lanes and the parity harness. Tests per host: bare `yin/def` in value position, as parameter, as key, computed key, wrong arity, `(yin/def 'yin/def _)` each refused at load on every loader including the semantic datom loader and the raw walker path; the same programs on all four backends produce identical stores; a lowered image with `:load-free yin/def` is refused; an old-stamped image with no definition is `:contract-mismatch`; an unstamped load is `:contract-missing`; a parked read inside a definition's value operand resumes in-process and writes the store, on the walker and semantic VM; redefining `x` twice reads the second value.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Change C, M2.** The four format records take the new contract names and the fetch requires one; the round 3 and 4 filters and `:yin-def?` bookkeeping go; the vector definitions scanner reads `:define`; obligations exclude the operator; the three fixtures and the alias fixture refuse `:reserved-name`; `(yin/def 'x 'yin/def)` links. Gate: the existing M2 gate, run once.\n    58\t\n    59\t**Moved to M4.** UCF frame encoding of a define frame, stamp refusal at lower, module-store snapshot lowering through `engine/store-put`, and the round-trip test.\n    60\t\n    61\t## 5. Docs and defects\n    62\t\n    63\tDocs, in addition to revision one's list: yin.vm.engine.md for the resolver rule and the single write function; yin.vm.semantic.md 2.6 for datom-side reserved operands and `:define`; code-as-tuples 7.5 for the vector rules; debruijn.stack.md for loader validation; linker.md 6.3 and 6.4 for the required contract; yin-repl-design for producer stamping; the UCF revisions log for the erratum and the four new revisions; datom.world.md gains one line under design principles naming the store-write lint.\n    64\t\n    65\tDefects, pre-existing and surfaced here: the stack loader admits without validation; no loader compares a contract; the M2 fetch admits an omitted contract. Deferred, unchanged: store-update discovery, advisory exports, host effects with computed keys under incomplete discovery, and everything listed under M4.\n     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 14:16:55 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 21:16:55 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\tRule R remains the right semantic direction, but the revised design is not yet a complete implementation gate. These statuses judge the proposed design; the checks have not been implemented.\n     5\t\n     6\t| Prior finding | Status | Evidence |\n     7\t|---|---|---|\n     8\t| Incoming macro store | RESOLVED | The revision checks both context construction and batch expansion, covering seeded and restored stores before `macro-of` can rewrite an application ([revision:18,51](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:18); [macro.cljc:973](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:973), [macro.cljc:1170](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1170), [macro.cljc:1220](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1220)). |\n     9\t| Direct admission | PARTLY RESOLVED | The walker’s datom, row, and raw-transition paths are now assigned checks, but the semantic datom-code loader is absent from the table ([revision:42](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:42); [semantic.cljc:670](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:670)). |\n    10\t| Effect and store ingress | PARTLY RESOLVED | Dispatcher, direct instructions, and constructors are covered. Portable module-store snapshots and restored scheduler updates still need an explicit check or an unreachability proof ([revision:36,52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:36); [linker spec:1342](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1342); [engine.cljc:423](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:423)). |\n    11\t| Four contract revisions | PARTLY RESOLVED | `v3/v3/b2/r2` covers the changed meanings, but validation alone does not reject every old lowered image, and direct loaders do not receive or compare a contract stamp ([revision:59-61](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:59); [semantic.cljc:713](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:713)). |\n    12\t| Quoted `yin/def` data | RESOLVED | The revised rule permits a literal symbol while refusing its use as a definition or store key ([revision:32-38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:32)). |\n    13\t\n    14\tActionable findings:\n    15\t\n    16\tP1 | [revision:47](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:47) | The table covers `code/well-formed-vector?`, but `semantic/vm-load-program` loads `:yin.code/*` datoms through the distinct `code/well-formed?` validator ([semantic.cljc:529](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:529), [code.cljc:162](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:162), [code.cljc:370](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:370)). That lane can otherwise admit `:var yin/def` or reserved store operands; its runtime `:var` still uses ordinary resolution ([semantic.cljc:263](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:263)). | Add semantic datom-code admission to the table and validate reserved operands and `:define` there, with a direct-loader regression test.\n    17\t\n    18\tP1 | [revision:36](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:36) | The rule forbids `yin/def` in a module-store snapshot, but the ten boundaries assign no check when a receiver instantiates snapshots; the linker spec explicitly restores them into `:module-stores` ([linker spec:1333](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1333), [linker spec:1342](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1342)). The scheduler can also merge `:store-updates` outside the listed store-write guards ([engine.cljc:404-425](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:404)). | Assign reserved-key validation to every portable-state lower/restore boundary and to admitted scheduler updates, or prove their keys are minted non-symbol IDs. Check carried environments as well as stores before execution. This is a design obligation for the deferred linker/UCF implementation, not a claim that today’s unimplemented module-snapshot path is exploitable.\n    19\t\n    20\tP1 | [revision:61](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:61) | “An old image contains `:var yin/def` or `:load-free yin/def`” is not generally true: an old image with no definition can pass the new grammar. The stack and register validators judge image structure, not an incoming version ([debruijn_code.cljc:846](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:846), [debruijn_register_code.cljc:667](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:667)); direct loaders take no contract argument ([semantic.cljc:728](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:728), [stack.cljc:95](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:95)). AST definition bytes can indeed be identical across revisions, so an old *stamped* AST manifest is refused by its stamp, not its body. The M2 fetch API currently accepts an omitted contract ([linker.cljc:1184-1193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:1184)). | Require and compare a contract at each persistent-code admission boundary for all four formats; update the optional wire/API contract and callers. Treat unversioned fresh source as an explicit separate path, not as proof that an old image was refused.\n    21\t\n    22\tP1 | [revision:91](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:91) | Criteria 7 and 10 require UCF lowering and a lift/lower round trip in the pre-M2 Rule R gate, but UCF is explicitly proposed/deferred and its current module provides canonicalization and safepoint helpers, not lift/lower ([UCF spec:1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1), [ucf.cljc:387](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc:387)). No VM loader compares an incoming UCF stamp today; the ledger’s derivation-profile comparison is a different check ([ledger.cljc:197](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:197)). | Gate Rule R now on the implemented loaders, contract mismatch, and a local parked-during-definition resume test. Keep UCF frame encoding and old-stamp lift/lower refusal as explicit M4 acceptance criteria, unless full UCF implementation is intentionally brought into this milestone.\n    23\t\n    24\tThe table otherwise places the walker, expander, de Bruijn resolver, and central effect-dispatch checks at sensible boundaries. `dao.await`’s post-constructor store merge uses newly minted keyword keys, not the symbol `yin/def` ([await.cljc:171-183](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/dao/await.cljc:171), [await.cljc:217-223](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/dao/await.cljc:217)); it merits a regression assertion but is not another demonstrated shadow route. A module-registry binding of that name likewise cannot change a syntactic definition if variable occurrences are rejected before resolution. The revision’s completeness condition is therefore **not yet proved**, principally because semantic datom admission and restored state are outside its stated boundaries.\n    25\t\n    26\tI would split the proposed two-commit sequence into three independently gated changes: first, compatible safety rails for reserved store keys and macro-context ingress; second, the atomic four-engine syntax, lowering, validation, and contract cutover; third, M2 guard removal and fixture changes. The full UCF round-trip remains a later milestone. This keeps each commit coherent without putting the known M2 alias-discharge defect on the committed branch. Add explicit tests for the semantic datom loader, snapshot rejection, an old no-`yin/def` image, and the raw walker’s reserved cases; criterion 1’s “one test per boundary” is otherwise too broad to prove those paths.\n    27\t\n    28\tVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\n    29\tSign-off: DENIED\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
